WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.767
Two computations that look unrelated: mixing numbers between
neighbours, and counting the solutions of an equation.

00:00:06.767 --> 00:00:14.167
Both reveal the same number, −2. This video explains why,
and shows the two small sums underneath.

00:00:14.167 --> 00:00:20.067
Start with a square. Switch each corner on or off:
16 markings.

00:00:20.067 --> 00:00:27.167
Rotating the square turns these four into each other.
They are one pattern, seen from four sides.

00:00:27.167 --> 00:00:35.667
Sorted by rotation, 6 patterns remain. A pattern&#x27;s symmetries are
the rotations that leave it unchanged, &#x27;no turn&#x27; included.

00:00:35.667 --> 00:00:46.867
Take one row: 4 copies, 1 symmetry. The product is 4,
the number of rotations. That is true of every row.

00:00:46.867 --> 00:00:54.667
So weigh each pattern by (1 ÷ symmetries), its share of the
four rotations, and sum the weights.

00:00:54.667 --> 00:01:02.067
The total is 16 ÷ 4 = 4, known before listing anything.
This weighted count is called the mass.

00:01:02.067 --> 00:01:10.667
Leave out this pattern and its weight disappears. The list now
accounts for only 3 of the expected mass of 4.

00:01:10.667 --> 00:01:19.267
That sum is the kernel&#x27;s first function: hand it the symmetry
counts and it adds their reciprocals as an exact fraction.

00:01:19.267 --> 00:01:27.567
Number theory has the same problem with harder objects. Each has
finitely many symmetries. We draw the first one found as A.

00:01:27.567 --> 00:01:34.967
A classical formula fixes the total mass in advance: 5/12.
A alone weighs 1/4, so something is still missing.

00:01:34.967 --> 00:01:41.467
The search finds B, with 6 symmetries. 1/4 + 1/6 = 5/12.
The total is reached.

00:01:41.467 --> 00:01:50.367
Provided the classes are valid, distinct and correctly weighted,
the list is complete. A and B stay with us from here on.

00:01:50.367 --> 00:01:58.267
To picture a neighbour, start with an integer lattice.
The marked point is the origin, with coordinates (0, 0).

00:01:58.267 --> 00:02:06.567
At the prime 2, keep half the lattice, including the origin.
The sum of any two retained points must also be retained.

00:02:06.567 --> 00:02:14.567
Try the odd columns. Their spacing is regular, but the origin
falls in the gap. This choice fails the sublattice test.

00:02:14.567 --> 00:02:22.267
Three index-two sublattices qualify. Each keeps the origin and
satisfies one of these parity rules.

00:02:22.267 --> 00:02:30.567
The left grid models one prime. Each parity rule picks out
one of its three neighbours at the prime 2.

00:02:30.567 --> 00:02:40.667
Why three? Each rule says that some combination a·x + b·y is even.
Mod 2 there are four pairs (a, b), and three of them are not zero.

00:02:40.667 --> 00:02:51.367
For another prime p, ask that a·x + b·y be divisible by p. Doubling
(a, b) gives the same rule, so at p = 3, 8 pairs make 4 rules.

00:02:51.367 --> 00:03:01.467
In general: p² − 1 nonzero pairs, p − 1 per rule, so p + 1 rules.
An object has p + 1 neighbours at the prime p.

00:03:01.467 --> 00:03:10.367
So &#x27;three neighbours at the prime 2&#x27; is 2 + 1. For our objects
the rule holds at every prime except 11.

00:03:10.367 --> 00:03:18.467
Now the real objects. Take one object of class A and build
its three neighbours at the prime 2.

00:03:18.467 --> 00:03:26.767
They are three different objects. But part 1 taught us to ask
a second question: which class does each one belong to?

00:03:26.767 --> 00:03:35.467
For A, one neighbour is equivalent to A itself and two are
equivalent to B. Different objects can share a class.

00:03:35.467 --> 00:03:43.233
Do the same for an object of class B: all three of its
neighbours are equivalent to A.

00:03:43.233 --> 00:03:51.833
The count of three came from the prime. These labels did not:
an upstream program, the workbench, has to compute them.

00:03:51.833 --> 00:03:58.633
Draw one arrow per neighbour, pointing at its class.
Three arrows leave A and three leave B.

00:03:58.633 --> 00:04:06.933
The loop at A does not mean &#x27;do nothing&#x27;. Doing nothing
would be a different picture, with a loop on every class.

00:04:06.933 --> 00:04:17.033
The loop is a real step whose result is equivalent to A. None of
B&#x27;s three steps comes back to B, so B has no loop.

00:04:17.033 --> 00:04:24.100
Now turn the arrows into numbers. Each class has its list of
three neighbours, already sorted into classes.

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:32.067
Build a table one cell at a time. Start at A. Mark each
neighbour that lands on B: 0, 1, 1.

00:04:32.067 --> 00:04:41.567
Add the marks, 0 + 1 + 1 = 2, and divide by a scale, here 1.
That is one call of the kernel&#x27;s second function.

00:04:41.567 --> 00:04:50.667
Every other cell is another call. The finished table is known
as the Brandt matrix. Assembling it is the caller&#x27;s job.

00:04:50.667 --> 00:04:59.533
There the scale was 1. In general it is built from parameters
the caller supplies, and the samples need not be 0 or 1.

00:04:59.533 --> 00:05:06.533
With fractions in, the answer is an exact fraction out,
never a rounded decimal.

00:05:06.533 --> 00:05:14.233
Now connect the two functions. A counts B twice among its
neighbours; B counts A three times. It looks lopsided.

00:05:14.233 --> 00:05:23.100
Weigh each side by its mass from part 1: 1/4 × 2 = 1/6 × 3.
The table is exactly balanced by the masses.

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:32.300
Call the two crossing counts n_AB and n_BA. Each has to be a
whole number from 0 to 3. Two pairs satisfy the balance equation.

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:42.500
The table has an edge from A to B. That rules out (0, 0).
The crossing counts must be 2 and 3, leaving one loop at A.

00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:50.500
Put any numbers a and b on A and B, and mix. Because of that
balance, the mass-weighted sum just triples.

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:59.667
So a weighted sum of zero stays zero. The patterns with
that property are the multiples of 2 on A, −3 on B.

00:05:59.667 --> 00:06:08.233
Keep A’s three destinations in place. Counting arrivals at B gives
0 + 1 + 1 = 2, one entry in the matrix.

00:06:08.233 --> 00:06:18.333
Now give A the value 2 and B the value −3. On the same three
destinations, the new values add to −4. That is the output at A.

00:06:18.333 --> 00:06:27.533
From B, all three neighbours land on A, so the second output is 6.
Compare the entire output vector with the one we put in.

00:06:27.533 --> 00:06:35.700
Both coordinates are multiplied by −2. This common scale factor
is the Hecke eigenvalue at the prime 2.

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:42.500
Now the other side: an equation that appears to have
nothing to do with our neighbour graph.

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:50.800
Work mod 2: x and y are 0 or 1, and even numbers vanish.
The equation simplifies, leaving four pairs to try.

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:56.667
Both sides are 0 whatever you plug in,
so all four pairs are solutions.

00:06:56.667 --> 00:07:05.233
The completed curve has one more point, at infinity: 5 in all.
An elliptic curve mod p has about p + 1 points.

00:07:05.233 --> 00:07:12.600
Expected 2 + 1, found 5. The gap is −2:
the same number the neighbour graph produced.

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:21.167
Why the same number? Both computations read one hidden object:
a modular form of level 11, written here as a power series.

00:07:21.167 --> 00:07:31.967
Call its coefficient of q² a₂. Both routes compute a₂ = −2:
one as a scale factor, one from point counts. It is a theorem.

00:07:31.967 --> 00:07:39.033
These bars compare curve counts with series coefficients.
We computed the neighbour table only at p = 2.

00:07:39.033 --> 00:07:47.633
They agree at every prime of good reduction: for this curve,
every prime except 11. The neighbour side matches by theorem.

00:07:47.633 --> 00:07:56.200
We used Gross’s table at level 11. The Rust example finds these edges
independently from the roots of a modular polynomial modulo 11.

00:07:56.200 --> 00:08:05.700
Divide each mass by 5/12 to get its share. Starting at A, two of
three edges cross. From B, all cross. The flows agree at 2/5.

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:15.267
Now export each destination just once. From A, the chance of crossing
drops to one half. The flows disagree: 3/10 one way, 2/5 the other.

00:08:15.267 --> 00:08:24.367
Run this example from the repository root. The kernel evaluates the
supplied lists exactly. The balance check exposes the lost edges.

00:08:24.367 --> 00:08:32.933
Counting B destinations gives the entry 2. Mixing the whole vector
gives the factor −2, the same number we found by counting points.

00:08:32.933 --> 00:08:41.433
Lean proves properties of the arithmetic on supplied tables.
The next film traces the two formulas through Alpöge’s Lean source.
